{"name":"Fiesta","hex":"#edd8d2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fiesta","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A religious festival.","A festive occasion."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To take part in a festive celebration; to party."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fiesta"}],"entries":[{"title":"Patronal festival","blocks":[{"text":"A patronal feast or patronal festival is a yearly celebration dedicated – in countries influenced by Christianity – to the 'heavenly advocate' or 'patron' of the location holding the festival, who is a saint or virgin. The day of this celebration is called patronal feast day, patronal day or patron day of said location."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patronal_festival"},{"title":"Fiesta San Antonio","blocks":[{"text":"Fiesta San Antonio has been since its 1891 inception an annual festival held in April in San Antonio, Texas. It is the city's signature event, along with some events held in the neighboring cities. The festival, also known as the Battle of Flowers, commemorates the Battle of the Alamo, which took place in San Antonio, and the Battle of San Jacinto, which led to Texas' independence from Mexico in April 1836."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Fiesta_San_Antonio_Logo.png/330px-Fiesta_San_Antonio_Logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiesta_San_Antonio"},{"title":"Fiesta (dinnerware)","blocks":[{"text":"Fiesta is a line of ceramic glazed dinnerware manufactured and marketed by the Fiesta Tableware Company of Newell, West Virginia, United States since its introduction in 1936, with a hiatus from 1973 to 1985. Fiesta is noted for its Art Deco styling and its range of often bold, solid colors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Fiesta_at_Macys_in_East_Wenatchee.jpg/330px-Fiesta_at_Macys_in_East_Wenatchee.jpg?utm_source=en.w